# cloudscheduler-jobs.yaml.example - Cloud Scheduler jobs for atomic-agents
#
# NOT YET DOGFOODED against the live GCP platform.
# Claims verified against GCP documentation as of 2026-06-09.
# See extras/gcp/README.md §External-claim verification status.
#
# Cloud Scheduler authenticates to Cloud Run using an OIDC token
# (Authorization: Bearer <token>), NOT the X-Goog-IAP-JWT-Assertion header
# that the serve layer reads for caller identity. As a result, scheduler-
# triggered calls have NO http_caller field in the JSONL audit record when
# Scheduler calls the run.app URL directly (see INGRESS RECONCILIATION below).
#
# To distinguish scheduled runs from interactive runs in a compliance audit:
# filter on trigger=http combined with the absence of http_caller, or
# correlate with Cloud Scheduler execution logs in Cloud Logging.
#
# PREREQUISITE: The Cloud Scheduler service account must have
# roles/run.invoker on the specific Cloud Run service:
#
#   gcloud run services add-iam-policy-binding <your-agent-name> \
#     --member=serviceAccount:SCHEDULER_SA@PROJECT.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
#     --role=roles/run.invoker \
#     --region=REGION
#
# If the scheduler job logs 403, verify the service account has
# roles/run.invoker on THIS specific Cloud Run service (not just project-level).
#
# INGRESS RECONCILIATION:
# cloudrun-service.yaml and iap-setup.md set
# run.googleapis.com/ingress = internal-and-cloud-load-balancing.
#
# Per the ingress docs, internal-and-cloud-load-balancing admits two source
# classes: (1) everything the more restrictive `internal` setting allows, PLUS
# (2) traffic via an external Application Load Balancer. The Google internal-
# traffic source list (Cloud Scheduler, Cloud Tasks, Pub/Sub, Eventarc,
# Workflows, ... when same-project or same VPC-SC perimeter) is defined under the
# `internal` setting and inherited here as the "(1)" class. So the doc-literal
# reading is that a same-project Scheduler job hitting the default run.app URL is
# admitted as internal traffic, and uses OIDC only (NO http_caller in the JSONL
# audit record).
# (Verified: the doc enumerates these sources under `internal` and states
#  internal-and-cloud-load-balancing = "resources allowed by the more restrictive
#  internal setting" + External ALB, at
#  https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/securing/ingress)
#
# OPERATIONAL CHECK (not a fabricated ambiguity, a real edge): the internal-
# source recognition is most crisply documented under the bare `internal` value.
# If same-project Scheduler-to-run.app returns 403/404 on your project under
# internal-and-cloud-load-balancing, the reliable fixes are (i) use topology (b)
# below - route Scheduler through the LB/IAP - or (ii) set ingress to bare
# `internal`. Topology (b) works unconditionally; prefer it if in doubt.
#
# Two topology options:
#   (a) SAME-PROJECT + DEFAULT run.app URL:
#       Doc-literal: Scheduler reaches Cloud Run directly via internal traffic.
#       No load balancer needed for scheduled calls.
#       Audit record: NO http_caller field (OIDC-only, not IAP-asserted).
#       Audience: the default run.app service URL (no trailing slash).
#       If this returns 403/404 on your project, switch to (b) or bare `internal`.
#   (b) CUSTOM DOMAIN or CROSS-PROJECT / VPC-SC perimeter (also the safe default):
#       Route Scheduler through the external load balancer / IAP instead.
#       The request then carries the X-Goog-IAP-JWT-Assertion header, so
#       the "no http_caller" note above no longer applies - scheduled calls
#       WOULD carry the IAP-asserted identity. Configure IAP to allow the
#       scheduler service account principal.
#       Audience: the load balancer / custom domain URL (not run.app).
#
# Pick one topology and keep cloudrun-service.yaml, iap-setup.md, and this
# file consistent.

jobs:
  - name: projects/<your-project-id>/locations/<region>/jobs/<your-agent-name>-daily
    description: "Daily run of <your-agent-name>"
    schedule: "0 9 * * *"
    timeZone: "America/Chicago"

    httpTarget:
      uri: "https://YOUR_CLOUD_RUN_SERVICE_URL/agents/<your-agent-name>/call"
      httpMethod: POST
      headers:
        Content-Type: "application/json"
      body: "eyJ3b3JrX2l0ZW0iOiAiRGFpbHkgcnVuIn0="
      # Body is base64-encoded JSON: {"work_item": "Daily run"}
      # work_item is the required request field (spec/37 §request shape);
      # there is no "prompt" alias - an absent/empty work_item returns HTTP 422.
      # Encode your own: echo -n '{"work_item": "..."}' | base64

      oidcToken:
        serviceAccountEmail: "SCHEDULER_SA@<your-project-id>.iam.gserviceaccount.com"
        # audience MUST match your Cloud Run service URL exactly.
        # Copy from Cloud Run console > Service URL. No trailing slash.
        # A mismatch causes HTTP 401 on every trigger (silent config error).
        # For topology (a) - same-project + run.app URL: use the run.app URL.
        # For topology (b) - via LB/IAP: use the load balancer / custom domain URL.
        audience: "https://YOUR_CLOUD_RUN_SERVICE_URL"

    retryConfig:
      # If agent.call() takes longer than the schedule interval, Cloud Run
      # returns HTTP 503 LockBusy and Cloud Scheduler retries. Set retryCount=0
      # or increase the schedule interval to prevent thundering-herd duplicate
      # trigger attempts against the same agent lock.
      retryCount: 0
      # Set maxDoublings and minBackoffDuration if you prefer retries with
      # backoff over no-retry. Ensure minBackoffDuration exceeds the expected
      # agent.call() runtime to avoid concurrent trigger pile-up.
      # minBackoffDuration: "300s"
      # maxDoublings: 1

    # Do not set min-instances=0 on the Cloud Run service while scheduler
    # triggers are active without confirming that the healthz endpoint responds
    # within the scheduler HTTP timeout. A cold start during the scheduler
    # window means the first invocation may time out before the instance warms,
    # triggering retries before the first run completes.
